  "notes": "The corpus's stress case for material binding. Its own criterion -- metal and roughness values -- needs the shading arm (board:1363) and is not claimed here. THIS CASE IS WHAT FOUND A FIXED NEAR PLANE (board:1420): its subject is 5.6 mm in radius and the framing rule puts the eye 40 mm away, so every vertex lay in front of the engine's then-fixed 0.05 m plane and NOTHING was drawn. The renderer now takes the near plane the placement declares and keeps the constant as a default, so a subject smaller than a matchbox renders at all.",
  "expected": {
    "subjectFrameFraction": {
      "value": 0.11852037442507703,
      "unit": "dimensionless",
      "origin": "derived",
      "derivation": "the projected area of the subject's triangles over the declared raster at the declared camera, recomputed by the runner from the subject's own vertices. Quoted because the boundary bound is applied UNDER it: I.26's IoU ~ 1 - P*d/A makes P/A a property of the subject's apparent size, so a camera framing the subject smaller would tighten the bound without saying so.",
      "note": "A consequence of the framing rule and the subject, not a choice. The runner refuses a mismatch."
    }
  }
